# Line endings are a build input, not a preference.
#
# The fixture gates pin sha256 over file BYTES, and the run receipt records
# source_sha256 over every tracked file. Git for Windows defaults
# core.autocrlf=true, so a plain checkout lands CRLF, git reports a clean
# status, and the same commit then hashes differently from the Linux receipt.
# These rules override that default for every clone on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.sas text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.sas7bdat binary
*.7bdat binary
*.deb binary
*.whl binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary

# FROZEN BYTES. Evidence, receipts and stamps are sha256-pinned, and a pinned
# receipt records what was measured at the time it was measured. Renormalizing
# one would invalidate the pin that cites it, so these paths take no end-of-line
# conversion at all: -text leaves every byte exactly as committed, on every
# platform, including the files that already carry CRLF or mixed endings.
# Measured 2026-11-25: 132 pinned files, 446 sha256 cross-references, all of
# them under the paths below. Operator ruling 2026-11-25: exempt, do not lift.
trials/** -text
kb/census/macro_surface_census.csv -text
references/rosetta-code/TASKS.csv -text
